from the  word amps  as we've seen Lebanese Arabic is  different in a number of ways from  Modern Standard Arabic some Lebanese  people go so far as to say that it's not  Arabic but rather a descendant of  Aramaic which has adopted Arabic  vocabulary the way English has adopted  many words from French to me it seems  like there's simply an Aramaic  substratum the influence of Aramaic as  its speakers began to adopt Arabic as  their main language it's interesting to  note that most Arabic dialects share a  lot of features in common that are  different from that suggests that Arabic  dialects didn't develop from fusa which  is literary but rather from a colloquial  form of Arabic which was used alongside  it in the 7th Century in any case  Lebanese and the other levantine  dialects are definitely fun and  interesting varieties of colloquial  Arabic and learning one of them  alongside Modern Standard Arabic would  definitely be a great choice if you're a  speaker of Lebanese Arabic or another  levantine dialect what other features or  words from your dialect would you like  us to know write your ideas in the  comments down below  if you're learning Arabic there's a  special and unique program that I think  you should check out it's called talk in  Arabic and it's the only place where you  can learn any of the major dialects of  spoken Arabic they have lessons  resources and HD videos covering each  variety so you can focus on whichever  variety you're learning but you can also  gain exposure to the other varieties  build an awareness of the differences  and improve your listening comprehension  of spoken Arabic in general to check out  talk in Arabic click the link in the  description you can use my code  langfocus for a 10 discount off any plan  I'd like to give a shout out to all of  the langfocus patreon supporters whose  support makes educational content like  this possible the people whose names you  see on the screen are the top tier  patreon supporters so let's 